  "markdown_report": "At 10% progress, prioritize **gated recent-interest residuals with uniform multi-negative cross-entropy**, subject to a controlled factorial comparison. No validation improvement is established.\n\n### Insights and directions\n\nThree starting-point insights guide the proposal. First, user feedback identifies stale-history dominance; a recent-interest branch can represent current activity while retaining SASRec’s historical context. This remains a hypothesis because perturbation probes completed no users. Second, broad resemblance often lacks a concrete reason to choose; learning directional next-item scores may help distinguish specific transitions from generic similarity, without establishing compatibility. Third, Electronics has 20.2% sampled training ranking errors and a −0.7499 tenth-percentile margin. These measurements motivate objective changes but do not predict held-out improvement.\n\nThree literature insights provide complementary directions. [TLSRec](https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.04760) motivates gated local/global fusion; its relevance here is architectural precedent, not benchmark evidence. [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/939) motivates testing the objective before expanding capacity; multi-negative CE offers a simple competing formulation. [SComGNN](https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.02130) distinguishes complementarity from similarity, motivating directional scoring while warning against interpreting observed succession as compatibility.\n\n### Framework\n\n| Direction | Existing approach | Minimal experiment | Remaining gap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Context | SASRec; TLSRec | Gated recent residual | Current intent unobserved |\n| Objective | BCE; gBCE; sampled CE | Uniform candidate CE | False negatives remain |\n| Relations | Complementary graphs | Asymmetric recent projection | Compatibility labels absent |\n\n### Candidate assessment\n\nScores are 0–10; higher difficulty means harder implementation. Each cell gives one strength (+) and limitation (−). U/M denote user/model diagnosis alignment.\n\n| Idea | Originality | Potential | U | M | Difficulty |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Uniform CE only | 2: +clean ablation; −established | 7: +reusable; −sampling-sensitive | 4: +sharper competition; −no recency mechanism | 8: +targets separation; −in-sample evidence | 2: +small change; −collision filtering |\n| Recent residual only | 3: +compact adaptation; −gating precedent | 7: +adaptive context; −window-sensitive | 8: +current specific transitions; −no compatibility guarantee | 5: +additional signal; −separation uncertain | 3: +small branch; −predictor synchronization |\n| **Combined** | 4: +joint mechanism; −incremental | 8: +factorial evidence; −extra search cost | 8: +stale and broad matches; −redundancy unresolved | 8: +candidate discrimination; −generalization unknown | 4: +modular; −four configurations |\n\n### Selected algorithm\n\nPreserve baseline normalization. At each valid position, pool the last five nonpadding item embeddings, project them asymmetrically, and add a small learned gated residual to SASRec features. This explicitly targets stale-history dominance and broad associations lacking specific current connections. CE targets Electronics’ observed sampled separation failures.\n\n```text\nr[t] = masked_mean(embedding(sequence[max(0,t-4):t+1]))\nf[t] = sasrec[t] + sigmoid(g([sasrec[t], r[t]])) * W(r[t])\nloss = CE(dot(f[t], embedding([positive, 64 uniform negatives])), 0)\n```\n\nExclude padding, known training interactions and the target from negatives; apply no LogQ correction. Initialize the gate near zero. Compare baseline, CE-only, residual-only and combined; select by pinned four-category macro validation HR@10, allowing baseline to win. Preserve seeds 0/42, effective 200 epochs, routing, `os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']`, single-GPU execution and trial-local artifacts. Fit experiments within the remaining shared budget. Synchronize predictor generation and diagnostic hooks; retain both required diagnostic callables, measured definitions and explicit dataset routing. Treat missing probes as unknown.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a small gated, asymmetrically projected recent-interest residual to per-dataset SASRec and train with uncorrected uniform multi-negative candidate cross-entropy. Select it only if the controlled comparison supports it.",
    "motivation": "The residual targets two user-facing failures: older isolated interactions outweighing current activity, and broad resemblance replacing specific current connections. Candidate CE targets the measured Electronics training separation weakness. Neither mechanism establishes compatibility or eliminates redundant recommendations.",
    "implementation_notes": "Preserve baseline feature normalization, separate models for all four datasets, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, and effective SASREC_EPOCHS default 200. Use a five-item causal masked mean, a bias-free learned projection, and a scalar sigmoid gate initialized near zero. Sample 64 uniform negatives per valid target, excluding padding, the target and known training interactions; use rejection sampling or an exact complement sampler when necessary. Do not use withheld interactions for exclusion. Preserve DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']; save configuration, checkpoints and the matching predictor beneath the trial directory. Compare the complete four-category baseline, objective-only, residual-only and combined configurations within the remaining 43,200 GPU-second shared budget; do not automatically select the combined model. Keep DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Expose the actual trained module, fused log2feats outputs and consistent predict_score behavior. Supply explicit dataset routing to probes and report unavailable perturbation results as unknown. Use only public histories, titles, descriptions and timestamps; no pretrained weights or unavailable metadata.",
    "pseudocode": "for variant in budget_feasible_factorial_variants:\n    for category in FOUR_REQUIRED_CATEGORIES:\n        initialize_with_training_seed_42()\n        for epoch in configured_epochs(default=200):\n            for _, sequence, positive, negative in train_loader:\n                h = baseline_sasrec_features(sequence)\n                if variant.uses_residual:\n                    r = causal_masked_recent_mean(item_emb(sequence), window=5)\n                    features = h + sigmoid(gate(concat(h, r))) * projection(r)\n                else:\n                    features = h\n                if variant.uses_ce:\n                    candidates = positive_plus_64_uniform_training_unseen_negatives()\n                    loss = masked_candidate_cross_entropy(features, candidates)\n                else:\n                    loss = unchanged_baseline_bce(features, positive, negative)\n                optimize(loss)\n        save_matching_configuration_and_checkpoint(category)\n    write_matching_predictor_with_all_category_routes()\n    obtain_pinned_macro_validation_hr10_if_budget_allows()\nselect_best_completed_configuration_including_baseline()",
    "originality": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "The compact combination links current-interest fusion and candidate competition to distinct observed failures and supports clean factorial attribution.",
      "negative": "Recent-interest gating and multi-negative objectives both have established precedents; the contribution is incremental."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 8,
      "positive": "The factorial comparison can establish whether context selection and objective changes provide complementary benefits before pursuing adaptive segmentation.",
      "negative": "Benefits remain unmeasured, and fixed-window pooling may blend unrelated recent activities."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "The branch and objective require modest changes to the existing SASRec model.",
      "negative": "Correct negative filtering, four-configuration budget management, predictor generation and diagnostic compatibility require coordinated implementation."
    }
  },
